A stack replaces recursive DFS here. Each stack item stores the node and the index of the next neighbor that still needs checking. State 1 means the node is active in the current stack path. Finding an edge to such a node is the cycle check.

This avoids recursive call depth, which is useful when the graph contains a long chain. The graph itself is only read, and no adjacency entries are changed.

```javascript
class Solution {
    isCyclic(N, adj) {
        const state = new Array(N).fill(0);

        for (let start = 0; start < N; start++) {
            if (state[start] !== 0) {
                continue;
            }

            const stack = [[start, 0]];
            state[start] = 1;

            while (stack.length > 0) {
                const top = stack[stack.length - 1];
                const node = top[0];
                const nextIndex = top[1];

                if (nextIndex === adj[node].length) {
                    state[node] = 2;
                    stack.pop();
                    continue;
                }

                const nextNode = adj[node][nextIndex];
                top[1]++;

                if (state[nextNode] === 1) {
                    return true;
                }

                if (state[nextNode] === 0) {
                    state[nextNode] = 1;
                    stack.push([nextNode, 0]);
                }
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(N + E)` since each adjacency entry is processed once.
Space complexity: `O(N)` for the state array and explicit stack.

Kahn's BFS removes nodes that cannot be part of a cycle. Start with all nodes whose indegree is zero. When a node is removed, reduce the indegree of its neighbors. If fewer than `N` nodes are removed, some nodes are still locked in a cycle.

Here is a small example: with `0 -> 1`, `1 -> 2`, and `2 -> 0`, every node has indegree one. The queue stays empty, so no node can be removed and the method returns `true`.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isCyclic(int N, List<List<Integer>> adj) {
        int[] indegree = new int[N];

        for (int node = 0; node < N; node++) {
            for (int next : adj.get(node)) {
                indegree[next]++;
            }
        }

        Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        for (int node = 0; node < N; node++) {
            if (indegree[node] == 0) {
                queue.offer(node);
            }
        }

        int removed = 0;
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int node = queue.poll();
            removed++;

            for (int next : adj.get(node)) {
                indegree[next]--;
                if (indegree[next] == 0) {
                    queue.offer(next);
                }
            }
        }

        return removed != N;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(N + E)`.
Space complexity: `O(N)` for indegrees and the queue. The indegree array is changed, but the adjacency list is not.

This is the direct version: start from each node and see whether that same node can be reached again by following directed edges. A fresh visited array is used for each start, so this method is easy to check but uses more time than the usual DFS or topological method.

**Edge cases:** an empty graph returns `false`, a graph with a self-loop returns `true`, and a graph with several disconnected components is handled because every node is used as a starting point.

```csharp
class Solution
{
    public bool IsCyclic(int N, List<List<int>> adj)
    {
        for (int start = 0; start < N; start++)
        {
            bool[] visited = new bool[N];
            Stack<int> stack = new Stack<int>();

            foreach (int next in adj[start])
            {
                if (next == start)
                {
                    return true;
                }

                if (!visited[next])
                {
                    visited[next] = true;
                    stack.Push(next);
                }
            }

            while (stack.Count > 0)
            {
                int node = stack.Pop();

                foreach (int next in adj[node])
                {
                    if (next == start)
                    {
                        return true;
                    }

                    if (!visited[next])
                    {
                        visited[next] = true;
                        stack.Push(next);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(N * (N + E))` in the worst case because a search can start from every node.
Space complexity: `O(N)` for the visited array and stack. This trades speed for a simple reachability check.

A color array keeps the DFS state of every node. State 0 means unvisited, state 1 means the node is on the current path, and state 2 means its outgoing edges are already done. Reaching a state 1 node means the current path has come back to itself.

**Dry run:** for edges `0 -> 1`, `1 -> 2`, and `2 -> 1`, DFS marks `0`, `1`, and `2` as active. The edge from `2` to `1` reaches an active node, so the function returns `true`.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    bool dfs(int node, vector<int> adj[], vector<int>& state) {
        state[node] = 1;

        for (int next : adj[node]) {
            if (state[next] == 1) {
                return true;
            }

            if (state[next] == 0 && dfs(next, adj, state)) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        state[node] = 2;
        return false;
    }

    bool isCyclic(int N, vector<int> adj[]) {
        vector<int> state(N, 0);

        for (int node = 0; node < N; node++) {
            if (state[node] == 0 && dfs(node, adj, state)) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(N + E)` because every node and edge is checked once.
Space complexity: `O(N)` for the state array and recursion stack.

This keeps a set of nodes on the current recursive path. A node may have been seen before in another finished search, and that is not enough to prove a cycle. It must be in the current path when reached again.

The code starts a search from every unvisited node, so disconnected parts of the graph are covered too. A self-loop such as `2 -> 2` is also caught because node 2 is already in the path when its neighbor is checked.

```python
class Solution:
    def isCyclic(self, N, adj):
        visited = [False] * N
        active = [False] * N

        def dfs(node):
            visited[node] = True
            active[node] = True

            for next_node in adj[node]:
                if not visited[next_node]:
                    if dfs(next_node):
                        return True
                elif active[next_node]:
                    return True

            active[node] = False
            return False

        for node in range(N):
            if not visited[node] and dfs(node):
                return True

        return False
```

Time complexity: `O(N + E)`.
Space complexity: `O(N)` for the two arrays and the recursion calls. Python recursion can be a limit for a very long one-way graph.

## Intuition
In a directed graph, a cycle exists if during DFS traversal we encounter a node that is already present in the current DFS path.

To track this efficiently, we use a single visited array with 3 states:

0 → Node is not visited

2 → Node is currently in the DFS path

1 → Node is fully processed (DFS completed)

While traversing, if we reach a node that is already marked as 2, it means we have found a back edge, which confirms the presence of a cycle.

## Approach

1) Create an integer array vis[] of size N initialized with 0.
2) Traverse all nodes of the graph.
3) For each unvisited node (vis[i] == 0), start a DFS traversal.
4) In DFS:
5 Mark the current node as 2 (path visited).

For every adjacent node:

If it is already marked as 2, a cycle is detected → return true.

If it is unvisited (0), recursively perform DFS.

After processing all neighbors, mark the node as 1 (DFS completed).
-  If no cycle is detected in any component, return false.  

**This approach ensures every node is visited once and cycles are detected efficiently.
**


## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public boolean isCyclic(int N, List<List<Integer>> adj) {
        int[] vis = new int[N];

        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            // 0 -> unvisited
            // 1 -> visited (completed)
            // 2 -> path visited
            if (vis[i] == 0) {
                if (dfs(i, vis, adj)) {
                    return true;
                }
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    private boolean dfs(int node, int[] vis, List<List<Integer>> adj) {

        // Mark current node as path visited
        vis[node] = 2;

        for (int it : adj.get(node)) {
            if (vis[it] == 2) {
                return true; // cycle found
            } 
            else if (vis[it] == 0) {
                if (dfs(it, vis, adj)) {
                    return true;
                }
            }
        }

        // Mark node as fully processed
        vis[node] = 1;
        return false;
    }
}


```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 

O(V + E)
Each node and edge is visited once during DFS traversal.


**Space Complexity:**
O(V)
Used for the visited array and recursion stack in the worst case.
